From 62e13edde2039e69f087174e500085f7fee84e30 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jgrizon
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 11:37:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 12] gpu: hash the host's address maps with the fold hasher, not
 SipHash

The submitting thread spent ~7 ms of each ~240 ms frame in the standard
hasher, in victory-gpu's maps of its own addresses: the list's twins,
texture states and widened states, the batch's pending and current
refreshes, the widened-ready set, the bound pipelines, the textures a list
brings up to date, the running submission's written copies, the page
table's view pair per copy, the tracker's saved pages, the recorder's lists
and ids, and the copies a give-up pass gathers. Their keys are addresses
and indices we choose (host copies, device pointers, slot numbers), never
text anyone picks, so the standard hasher's protection against chosen keys
buys nothing there and its multiply-and-rotate costs the submitting thread.

census_maps gains FoldHashing (its multiply-and-fold hasher alone, as a
zero-sized BuildHasher) and the FoldMap/FoldSet aliases; the census's own
maps are untouched, and maps keyed by strings or by tuples that are not
addresses keep the standard hasher.

No VICTORY_* flag: the maps hold the same entries and answer every question
the same way, only the hash differs. Nothing reads a map's iteration order
(the one count that wants order sorts itself), so no answer changes.

Generated by Mistral Vibe.
Co-Authored-By: Mistral Vibe <vibe@mistral.ai>
